---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const conditional = `// Throws when open is false
css\`width: \${open && width}px;\`;

// Uses the default when open is false
css\`width: var(\${open ? width : null}px, 120px);\`;`;

const parsed = `// Throws when the prop isn't a number, because parseInt returns NaN
css\`width: \${parseInt(size)}px;\`;

// Uses the default instead
const width = parseInt(size);
css\`width: var(\${Number.isNaN(width) ? null : width}px, 120px);\`;`;

const object = `// Throws: theme is an object
css\`color: \${theme};\`;

// Pass the value you want from it
css\`color: \${theme.color};\`;`;
---

<Layout
  title="Error 1: invalid runtime value | pre.style"
  description="A runtime value in a pre.style block wasn't a string, a finite number, null, or undefined."
>
  <main>
    <p><a href="/errors">Runtime errors</a></p>
    <h1>Error 1: invalid runtime value</h1>
    <p>
      <code>{'pre.style error 1 (<type>): https://pre.style/errors/1'}</code>
    </p>
    <p>
      A <code>{'${…}'}</code> in a <code>css</code> or <code>scss</code> block held a value pre.style
      can't use. The message's <code>{'<type>'}</code> is the value's type, from
      <code>typeof</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>What values can be</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A string or a finite number, which sets the value.</li>
      <li>
        <code>null</code> or <code>undefined</code>, which adds no class, so the block's default
        applies.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Everything else throws: <code>true</code> and <code>false</code>, <code>NaN</code> and
      <code>Infinity</code>, objects, arrays, functions, symbols, and bigints.
    </p>

    <h2>Why it throws</h2>
    <p>
      These values are almost always bugs. Using the default instead would hide the bug, and the
      page would only look slightly wrong. It throws on the server and in the browser, so the two
      always agree.
    </p>

    <h2>Common causes</h2>

    <h3><code>boolean</code>: a condition with <code>&&</code></h3>
    <p>
      <code>{'open && width'}</code> is <code>false</code> when <code>open</code> is false. Use
      <code>null</code> for "no value".
    </p>
    <pre><code>{conditional}</code></pre>

    <h3><code>number</code>: <code>NaN</code> or <code>Infinity</code></h3>
    <p>
      Ordinary numbers are fine, so this type means the value wasn't finite. It usually comes from
      parsing text that isn't a number, doing math on <code>undefined</code>, or dividing by zero.
    </p>
    <pre><code>{parsed}</code></pre>

    <h3><code>object</code> or <code>function</code>: the wrong variable</h3>
    <p>Check that the expression is the value itself, rather than what holds it.</p>
    <pre><code>{object}</code></pre>

    <h2>Finding the value</h2>
    <p>
      Run a development build. Its message says which value failed and shows the text around it,
      and suggests a fix. The stack trace points to the component in either build.
    </p>

    <h2>Not this error</h2>
    <p>
      A string of the wrong kind, such as <code>"red"</code> for a width, doesn't throw, because
      pre.style can't tell. The browser resets the declaration. To use the default instead, give the
      value a type:
      <code>{'var(${w}px type(<length>), 120px)'}</code>.
    </p>
  </main>
</Layout>
